course: religion-ethics
lesson: 93
title: Cheating at games and sport
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A game is fun because everyone tries to win inside the same rules; cheating takes away the very thing that makes it a game.
explain: At sports day Kieran starts before the whistle and his egg is stuck to the spoon with Blu Tack; he may get the ribbon, but not the real win, because the skill and the joy were never his.
explain: Rules agreed before a game are a kind of promise between players, so changing them when you're losing, peeking at cards or using hacks breaks that promise.
explain: Paul wrote that an athlete does not receive "the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules" (2 Timothy 2:5), and fair play awards go to players who tell the referee the truth.
explain: You can call out cheating kindly, and with little children, games are where they learn fairness, so gently remind them of the rules.
fact: Cheating at a family game "for a laugh" spoils what makes the game fun; Proverbs 26:18-19 warns against the one who deceives a neighbour and says, "I was only joking!"
fact: "Anyone who competes as an athlete does not receive the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules" (2 Timothy 2:5).
fact: Maradona's "Hand of God" goal in 1986 went in off his hand and is still argued about; fair play awards go to players who correct a referee.
fact: Game hacks like aimbots are cheating real people out of a fair game, so game companies ban them; "Love your neighbour as yourself" (Mark 12:31).
fact: "Start children off on the way they should go" (Proverbs 22:6): young children learn fair play by being gently reminded of the rules.
fact: "Do not be deceived: God cannot be mocked" (Galatians 6:7); a cheat can get the medal but never the joy of the real skill.
fact: Lance Armstrong won the Tour de France seven times and lost all seven titles in 2012 for doping (taking banned drugs to race faster); "You may be sure that your sin will find you out" (Numbers 32:23).
fact: Call out cheating calmly and privately: "Go and point out their fault, just between the two of you" (Matthew 18:15).
fact: In cricket "walking" means a batter who knows he's out leaves without waiting for the umpire; not every player walks, and people argue about it.
fact: "Do not use dishonest standards when measuring length, weight or quantity" (Leviticus 19:35): changing the rules mid-game to win is cheating.
fact: Letting little ones win sometimes keeps them keen; older children usually want real wins, and can ask to play properly.
fact: "Ye might fool the ref, but ye'll no fool yersel": the cheat loses skill, pride and trust; "In humility value others above yourselves" (Philippians 2:3).
fact: Slang check: "yaar" (India) means mate; "fuming" means very angry; "ye'll no fool yersel" (Scotland) means you won't fool yourself.
exercise: Grandad hides Monopoly money under the board "for a laugh". The kids are fuming. Why? | Cheating spoils what makes the game fun | Because Grandad is winning fairly | Because Monopoly has too many rules | Because they wanted to hide money first
exercise: What does "fuming" mean? | Very angry | Very happy | Very tired | Very hungry
exercise: Freya's goal went in off her hand and the ref didn't see. What should she do? | Tell the ref; a goal she didn't really score isn't worth having | Celebrate, because the ref is always right | Say nothing and score another one | Blame a defender for the handball
exercise: What does 2 Timothy 2:5 say about athletes? | No crown except by competing according to the rules | Winning is all that matters in the end, however you do it | Athletes should never train or play games on Sundays | The fastest runner always gets the crown, rules or no rules
exercise: A kid says, "The aimbot's not even cheating, it's skill." Is it cheating? | Yes; it cheats real people out of a fair game | No; it's just a clever tool that good players use | No; online games have no rules, so anything goes | Only if you win every single game you play with it
exercise: Ava, 5, peeks while counting in hide and seek. What is the kind response? | Gently remind her of the rule | Shout "Cheat!" and stop playing | Ignore it, little kids can't learn rules | Peek back at her to be fair
exercise: Kieran's egg is stuck on with Blu Tack and he started early. Is it a real win? | No; he gets the ribbon but not the real win | Yes; a ribbon is a ribbon | Yes, because nobody noticed | No, but only because Blu Tack is not allowed
exercise: Why did Lance Armstrong lose his seven Tour de France titles? | He was caught doping, taking banned drugs to race faster | He fell off his bike in the very last race and could not finish | He was too old to keep his titles, so the rules took them back | He gave them all away to other riders because he felt sorry
exercise: Ella keeps "accidentally" seeing Ruby's cards at Uno. How can Ruby call it out kindly? | Hey, I can see you peeking. Hold yours up and I'll hold mine up? | Cheat! Everyone, Ella is a cheat! | Peek at Ella's cards as well, to get even with her and win the game anyway | Stop playing with Ella for ever
exercise: Arjun is clearly caught at cricket but shouts, "Not out, yaar!" What does "yaar" mean? | Mate | Yes | Never | Umpire
exercise: What is "walking" in cricket? | Leaving when you know you're out, without waiting for the umpire | Walking slowly between the wickets | Walking off the pitch when it rains | Refusing to bat until it's your turn
exercise: Max changes the rules of his game whenever he's losing. What is fair? | Agree the rules first and stick to them | Let the person who is losing make new rules | Change the rules every five minutes for everyone | Play without any rules at all
exercise: Grandma lets Liam win at chess. Is that cheating? | For little ones it's fine; at 9 he can ask her to play properly | Yes; Grandma is a cheat, and she should be told off in front of everyone | No; Grandma should always let him win | Yes; letting someone win is the worst lie
exercise: Coach says, "Ye might fool the ref, but ye'll no fool yersel." What does he mean? | You can trick the ref, but not yourself | You'll never be able to trick a referee at all | Yourself is the best referee you could ever have | Fooling the ref is a good skill to learn
exercise: How does cheating hurt the cheat? | They lose skill, pride and trust | They get better at the game faster | They make more friends on the team | It doesn't; only others get hurt
test: Your mate says, "Grandad cheating at Monopoly is just a joke, chill." What does Proverbs 26:18-19 say about that? | It warns against deceiving someone and then saying "I was only joking!" | It says jokes in games are always fine | It says only children's cheating counts | It says Monopoly is a sinful game
test: "Crap, the ref missed it, keep schtum!" shouts Freya's teammate after her handball goal. ("Keep schtum" means keep quiet.) What is best? | Tell the ref anyway; a fair-play goal is the only one worth having | Keep quiet; refs are paid to see everything, so it's their own fault | Score again so it doesn't matter | Shout at the ref for missing it
test: A cartoon shows a hero who wins a race by tying the others' laces together and gets a gold medal. What is wrong? | A win by a trick isn't a real win | Nothing; the hero was just clever | The hero should have tied more laces | Laces don't matter in real races
test: "Bro, everyone uses hacks, you're a noob if you don't," says a gamer. (A "noob" is a beginner.) What is the best reply? | Hacks ruin the game for real people; I'd rather play fair | Fine, I'll use them too, so nobody calls me a noob ever again | Only use hacks against noobs | Report yourself to the game company
test: What would happen if every runner could start before the whistle? | Races would mean nothing, and nobody would know who's fastest | Races would be more exciting and fair, because anyone could win | Everyone would get a medal | Nothing; whistles are only decoration
test: Ella says, "I wasn't peeking, it was an accident... five times." What would happen if Ruby shouts "Cheat!" in front of everyone? | It may start a fight; calling it out calmly and privately works better | Ella would immediately say sorry | Everyone would agree Ruby is right, and Ella would never ever cheat again | Nothing; shouting always fixes cheating
test: Your cousin says, "Armstrong still won seven times, so cheating paid off." What is wrong? | He lost all seven titles and his good name; cheats steal from honest riders | Nothing; he kept all his trophies in the end | He was never caught for anything | Doping is allowed in cycling
test: Max yells, "My game, my rules, I just changed them, I win!" What is the problem? | Rules agreed first are a promise; changing them to win breaks it | Nothing; the person who invents a game always wins | He should change them back only if he wins | Games are more fun without any rules
test: Liam, 9, says, "Gran, play properly, I want a real win." What does this show? | He's ready for real wins that he earned | He is being rude to his gran by telling her what to do | He doesn't like chess any more and wants to stop | He wants Gran to cheat even more than before
test: In street cricket Arjun walks off when he's caught, without arguing. What do people think of "walking"? | It's hard and admirable, though not every player does it | It's silly, because umpires decide everything and players shouldn't | It's against the rules of cricket, so he could be sent off for it | It means he gave up because he was tired and wanted to go home
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the effect of cheating upon a game? | It removes the very constraints which render the game meaningful and enjoyable | It enhances the enjoyment of all participants equally | It is of no consequence where no prize is awarded | It renders the game more challenging for the cheat
exam: Select the statement consistent with 2 Timothy 2:5. | An athlete receives the victor's crown only by competing according to the rules | An athlete receives the victor's crown irrespective of adherence to the rules of the contest | An athlete forfeits the crown upon competing according to the rules | The crown is awarded solely by the vote of spectators
exam: Identify the cricketing practice whereby a batter who knows himself to be out departs without awaiting the umpire's decision. | Walking | Bowling | Fielding | Appealing
exam: Given that a young child peeks whilst counting in a game of hide and seek, ascertain the response most consistent with this lesson. | A gentle reminder of the rule, games being the means whereby children learn fairness | The immediate termination of the game and public censure of the child | The imitation of the child's conduct by all other players | The disregard of the matter, rules being unintelligible to children
